        methods:
          "The interval $[0,T]$ is divided into $m = T/h$ time steps and the forward Euler method is applied to the Carleman system, $y^{k+1} = [I + A(kh)h]y^k + b(kh)$ with $y^0 = y_{in} = \\hat{y}(0)$, and all $y^k$ are then held equal for $k$ beyond $m$ for a sufficiently large integer $p$ of padding steps. That gives the $(m+p+1)\\Delta \\times (m+p+1)\\Delta$ system $L|Y\\rangle = |B\\rangle$ with $L = \\sum_{k=0}^{m+p}|k\\rangle\\langle k| \\otimes I - \\sum_{k=1}^{m}|k\\rangle\\langle k-1| \\otimes [I + A((k-1)h)h] - \\sum_{k=m+1}^{m+p}|k\\rangle\\langle k-1| \\otimes I$, which the paper notes is lower triangular, and $|B\\rangle$ assembled from $\\lVert y_{in} \\rVert |0\\rangle \\otimes |y_{in}\\rangle$ and the terms $\\lVert b((k-1)h) \\rVert |k\\rangle \\otimes |b((k-1)h)\\rangle$ with a normalizing factor $B_m$. The step is not free: (4.46) fixes $h \\le \\min\\{1/(N\\lVert F_1 \\rVert),\\, 2(|\\mathrm{Re}(\\lambda_1)| - \\lVert F_2 \\rVert - \\lVert F_0 \\rVert)/(N(|\\mathrm{Re}(\\lambda_1)|^2 - (\\lVert F_2 \\rVert + \\lVert F_0 \\rVert)^2 + \\lVert F_1 \\rVert^2))\\}$, or $h \\le 1/(N\\lVert F_1 \\rVert)$ when the eigenvalues of $F_1$ are all real, and the discussion says the point of that choice is to ensure $\\lVert I + Ah \\rVert \\le 1$. Under it Lemma 3 bounds the global Euler error by $\\lVert \\hat{y}(T) - y^m \\rVert \\le 3N^{2.5}Th[(\\lVert F_2 \\rVert + \\lVert F_1 \\rVert + \\lVert F_0 \\rVert)^2 + \\lVert F_0' \\rVert]$, linear in $T$ rather than the $e^{LT}$ a general Lipschitz argument would give.",
